<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Renfrew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1164">1164</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Renfrew was fought in 1164 near Renfrew on the Clyde, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a>, King of the Isles, invaded the Scottish kingdom with a large fleet drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, the Isles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. Somerled was killed early in the engagement, possibly by treachery, and his army dispersed. His death left his lands divided among his sons, ancestors of the MacDougalls, MacDonalds and MacRuairis.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Perhaps about 1140, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a> married Ragnhildr, daughter of Óláfr Guðrøðarson, King of the Isles, which gave his descendants, Clann Somairle, a claim to the kingship. After Óláfr's assassination in 1153 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godred-olafsson">Guðrøðr Óláfsson</a> took the throne, but Somerled mounted a coup in his own son Dubgall's name, fought Guðrøðr to a draw at sea and divided the kingdom with him, then drove him into exile two years later. In 1153 Somerled had also backed the sons of Máel Coluim mac Alasdair, a claimant to the Scottish throne, in a failed rising against the Crown.</p><p>Why he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1164 is uncertain. He may have been renewing his support for Máel Coluim's sons, or seeking to win back the south-west, lands once held by the Gall Gaidheil that had been granted to Scottish magnates such as Walter fitz Alan, Steward of Scotland. The illness of Malcolm IV, King of Scotland, struck by a great sickness in 1163, may have encouraged him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to the Chronicle of Mann, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a>'s fleet numbered one hundred and sixty ships, which at forty to fifty men a ship would mean six to eight thousand men, though medieval tallies are suspect. Irish annals say his forces came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a>, the Isles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. The fleet made landfall at Renfrew, a seat of Walter's lordship, perhaps at Inchinnan. The Carmen de Morte Sumerledi suggests that Somerled was killed at the outset by hastily gathered local defenders, that his head was cut off by a priest and presented to Herbert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, and that his leaderless men gave up the fight. A son, named Gilla Brigte in the Annals of Tigernach, died with him. The Scottish commander is unrecorded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Guðrøðr's brother Rǫgnvaldr briefly seized Mann, but Guðrøðr overcame him within the year, and the Isles were again divided between Guðrøðr and Clann Somairle. The victory appears to have secured peace for the rest of Malcolm's reign, and it is the last major event of that reign on record. In 1772 Thomas Pennant was shown a mound with a single stone at Knock, between Renfrew and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, as the place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a>'s defeat; the monument, known as Queen Blearie's Stone, was demolished before the end of the eighteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Renfrew” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Somerled was killed in 1164 at <b>Renfrew</b> while invading the Kingdom of Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-argyll-and-the-isles">Kingdom of Argyll and the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
